{"author":{"id":"a3a282966ced55effb4560271fcd98e13241af24ea76b00ec76a0f18154bc8ee"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790192138,"nonce":"7ThpbW3P5dlRqXIBBFgaZQL8l9k=","message":"Revised 2026-09-23 (Luis): a flow's script is exactly one Starlark function definition. Its name is the flow key, its docstring the description (mirrored into the attribute by import), its parameters the arguments with defaults; import reads all three from the syntax tree without executing, and rejects a second statement, a load, or no def. Starlark has no annotations, so types come from defaults; richer typing is the future step formerly called args. The file's name and location do not matter: `flow import` takes any files, directories or stdin (a scratch file in /tmp works) and is an upsert; archiving what a directory no longer has is `--prune`. Run with `git work flow run NAME '{\"kw\": ...}'` where the JSON object is the keyword arguments, which is exactly `flow.run(name, **kwargs)`. `--gui` renders the returned spec in the browser instead of the terminal. Full map: doc/design/cli-convention.md.","files":null}]}